        <Sticky classid="Sticky" id="1479332660" x="288" y="108" w="846" h="558" colors="0, 15792890">
          <paramlist>
            <param name="full_font" value="Tahoma, 10pt" />
            <param name="align" value="0" />
            <param name="fg_color_adv" value="#FF000000" />
            <param name="bg_color_adv" value="#FFFFFFFF" />
            <param name="hl_color_adv" value="#FFFFFF00" />
            <param name="bdr_color_adv" value="#FF808080" />
            <param name="arrows" value="0" />
            <param name="style" value="1" />
            <param name="font" value="1" />
            <param name="fg_color" value="0" />
            <param name="bg_color" value="15792890" />
          </paramlist><![CDATA[This case compares the modeling approaches at different fidelity levels for the power electronic modules in DC_EMT_lib.pslx. For the IT load, the time-domain simulation consists of three stages:

1. Initialization (t = 0–2 s): The IT load is initialized to 0.6 pu.

2. Grid-side disturbance (t = 2 s): A voltage dip is applied at t = 2 s, and the voltage begins to recover at t = 2.5 s.

3. Workload-side disturbance (t = 6 s onward): A periodic square-wave variation is applied to the IT workload to represent power demand fluctuations associated with AI training tasks.

These grid-side and workload-side disturbances allow comparison of the responses of the average, switching, and reduced-order models.

For the cooling loads, the model is initialized during the first 5 s. The same voltage-dip profile used for the IT load is then applied at t = 5 s to compare the responses of the average and switching models to a grid-side disturbance.

These comparisons highlight how modeling fidelity affects the simulated dynamic responses and provide insights into model selection for different study objectives.

]]></Sticky>
